$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: Representative experimental procedure for the
reaction of DAST with epoxy alcohols: Epoxy alcohol (1 mmol) was taken in
a dry 10 mL round bottom flask and 2 mL of pentane was added to dissolve
the compound completely. DAST (150 μL, 1.1 eq) was slowly added under N2.
Reaction was stirred at room temperature for 15 min and was quenched by
adding saturated aqueous Na2CO3 solution (3 mL). The organic layer was
separated and dried over MgSO4. The pentane was distilled off via short
path distillation set-up, and the residue was purified by column
chromatography on SiO2 using pentane and ether as eluents. The fractions
containing the desired compounds were pooled and the solvent was
distilled off at atmospheric pressure. The product obtained was analyzed
by NMR, and the results were in agreement with the previous experiment.
In such cases where the compounds were stable enough, samples were
submitted for mass spectral analysis. For the exemplification of this
methodology, we have provided all the copies of NMR spectra (crude
reaction mixture and isolated compounds) obtained from the experiments on
compounds of cyclooctene oxide series. Reaction of syn-epoxy alcohol 3e
with DAST : Since this reaction gave a single product 5e, crude reaction
mixture was washed with saturated aqueous Na2CO3 solution and passed
through a short pad of SiO2 to obtain pure compound (95 mg, 73%). 1H NMR
(400 MHz, CDCl3, ppm) δ 3.35-3.28 (m, 1H), 3.12 (ddt, 1H, J = 4.1, JHF =
1.6, J = 0.8 Hz), 2.00-1.40 (m, 6H), 1.52 (d, 3H, JHF = 21.7 Hz); 13C NMR
(100 MHz, CDCl3, ppm) δ 93.6 (d, J = 171.7 Hz), 56.2 (d, J = 21.9 Hz),
54.3 (d, J = 5.2 HZ), 32.1 (d, J = 23.3 Hz), 23.9 (d, J = 25.7 Hz), 22.6
(d, J = 1.2 Hz), 18.2 (d, J = 6.8 Hz); 19F NMR (376 MHz, CDCl3, ppm) δ
-142.3 (qddd, J = 21.7, 15.7, 4.2, 1.6 Hz).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM A Facile C-C Bond Cleavage in the Epoxides and Its Use for the Synthesis
of Oxygenated Heterocycles by a Ring Expansion Strategy
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Lakshmipathi, Pandarinathan; Gree, Danielle; Gree, Rene
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2002), 4(3), 451-454

